INSTALLATION PROCEDURE
======================

This document explains how to build, install and configure the SpringCard CCID
TCP IFD Handler for pcsc-lite.

This is a network driver for SpringCard readers. It is not the USB CCID driver,
and it does not use USB device discovery.


Installation from source:
"""""""""""""""""""""""""

From a release archive:

    $ tar xzvf springcard_ccid_tcp-x.y.z.tar.gz
    $ cd springcard_ccid_tcp-x.y.z
    $ ./configure
    $ make
    $ sudo make install

From a checkout, run the project bootstrap step first if configure has not yet
been generated:

    $ ./bootstrap
    $ ./configure
    $ make
    $ sudo make install

The configure script obtains pcsc-lite paths with pkg-config. The main driver
bundle is installed below the pcsc-lite drop directory returned by:

    $ pkg-config libpcsclite --variable=usbdropdir

With the default bundle name, the installed driver directory is:

    $(pkg-config libpcsclite --variable=usbdropdir)/ifd-springcard-ccid-tcp.bundle

make install also installs an empty, commented reader configuration example in
the pcsc-lite reader configuration directory returned by:

    $ pkg-config libpcsclite --variable=serialconfdir

The installed example is named:

    springcard-ccid-tcp.conf

The installed shared library can be found with:

    $ find "$(pkg-config libpcsclite --variable=usbdropdir)" \
        -path "*/ifd-springcard-ccid-tcp.bundle/Contents/*/libspringcard_ccid_tcp.*" \
        -print

If your system uses non-standard pcsc-lite paths, pass them explicitly to
configure. The most common option is:

    $ ./configure --enable-usbdropdir=/path/to/pcsc/drivers

The option name is inherited from pcsc-lite/CCID conventions. In this project it
selects the pcsc-lite IFD Handler drop directory; it does not mean that the
driver uses USB.

To override the directory where the commented reader configuration example is
installed, use:

    $ ./configure --enable-ethernetconfdir=/path/to/reader.conf.d


Dependencies:
"""""""""""""

Build dependencies include:

- a C compiler and standard build tools
- autoconf/automake/libtool when building from a checkout
- pkg-config
- pcsc-lite development headers and library
- POSIX threads
- libusb development files, unless configured out with --disable-libusb

Some libusb checks are still present because this project is derived from the
CCID driver build system. The runtime transport used by this driver is TCP.


Configuring network readers:
""""""""""""""""""""""""""""

Declare each network reader in a file under the pcsc-lite reader configuration
directory returned by:

    $ pkg-config libpcsclite --variable=serialconfdir

This is typically /etc/reader.conf.d. Current pcsc-lite systems use files in
that directory for manually configured IFD Handlers; do not document or rely on
editing the old global /etc/reader.conf file for new installations.

make install creates the file:

    $(pkg-config libpcsclite --variable=serialconfdir)/springcard-ccid-tcp.conf

It is intentionally empty from pcscd's point of view because every sample line
is commented. Edit this file, uncomment one reader block and adapt it to your
site.

A typical entry is:

    FRIENDLYNAME      "E663"
    DEVICENAME        192.168.17.100:3999:00:nokey
    LIBPATH           /usr/local/pcsc/drivers/ifd-springcard-ccid-tcp.bundle/Contents/Linux/libspringcard_ccid_tcp.so
    CHANNELID         0

Adapt the IP address, TCP port and LIBPATH to your installation. The generated
springcard-ccid-tcp.conf example already contains the LIBPATH selected by
configure. You may also use the find command above to get the exact installed
library path.

CHANNELID is kept in the example for compatibility with pcsc-lite's
reader.conf syntax, but recent IFD Handler 3.0 drivers use DEVICENAME to pass
the actual endpoint to the driver.

DEVICENAME format:

    host:port:option:key

This legacy positional syntax is still supported. The fields are:

- host: reader host name or IPv4 address
- port: reader TCP port, from 1 to 65535
- option: reader option byte, from 0 to 255; decimal and 0x-prefixed
  hexadecimal values are accepted
- key: reader key string, for example nokey when no key is used

Legacy example:

    192.168.17.100:3999:00:nokey

The newer optional named syntax is:

    host=<host>:port=<port>:options=<options>:key=<key>:slots=<slots>

Named fields may be omitted when the default value is suitable:

- port defaults to 3999
- options defaults to 0x00
- key defaults to an empty string
- slots defaults to 1 and may be set from 1 to 4

The host is mandatory. In named mode, it may be written either as host=<host> or
as the first unnamed token:

    host=192.168.17.100:port=3999:options=0x00:key=nokey:slots=1
    192.168.17.100:slots=4

The configuration may also be moved to a separate INI-style file:

    file=<filename>[:section=<section>]

Example DEVICENAME:

    file=/etc/springcard-ccid-tcp/readers.conf
    file=/etc/springcard-ccid-tcp/readers.conf:section=front-desk

Example external configuration:

    [front-desk]
    host=192.168.17.100
    port=3999
    options=0x00
    key=nokey
    slots=1
    hook=/usr/local/sbin/springcard-reader-hook

Supported external configuration fields:

- host: reader host name or IPv4 address, mandatory
- port: TCP port, default 3999
- options: reader option byte, default 0x00
- key: reader key string, default nokey
- slots: number of slots exposed by this reader, default 1
- hook: availability hook script, default springcard-ccid-tcp-hook from the
  installed driver bundle

When section= is omitted, the file may contain plain key=value lines. If it
contains sections, the first section is used.

After changing the reader configuration file, restart pcscd.

Important for pcsc-lite 2.2.0 and later: upstream pcsc-lite now uses Meson, and
its serial reader support is disabled by default unless pcsc-lite is configured
with -Dserial=true. Distribution packages may enable it for you. If pcscd does
not parse the serialconfdir directory, this driver will install correctly but no
network reader will be created.


Finding the availability hook:
""""""""""""""""""""""""""""""

The hook script is installed in the driver bundle. Locate it with:

    $ find "$(pkg-config libpcsclite --variable=usbdropdir)" \
        -path "*/ifd-springcard-ccid-tcp.bundle/Contents/Resources/springcard-ccid-tcp-hook" \
        -print

With default paths, it is usually:

    $(pkg-config libpcsclite --variable=usbdropdir)/ifd-springcard-ccid-tcp.bundle/Contents/Resources/springcard-ccid-tcp-hook

The driver calls the hook with two arguments:

    springcard-ccid-tcp-hook "<reader name>" available
    springcard-ccid-tcp-hook "<reader name>" unavailable

The hook is optional. If it is not executable, the driver silently skips it.


Hook constraints:
"""""""""""""""""

The hook runs from the pcscd/driver context and must be treated as privileged
operational code.

Security requirements:

- keep the script owned and writable only by a trusted administrator
- do not use untrusted input to build shell commands
- quote all arguments
- use absolute paths for called programs when possible
- keep PATH assumptions minimal
- avoid storing secrets directly in the script

Runtime requirements:

- return quickly; the driver enforces a 5 second timeout
- do not perform long network operations directly
- hand off slow work to a supervised service, queue or monitoring agent
- do not write to stdout or stderr for normal operation; they are redirected to
  /dev/null by the driver
- make the script idempotent because reader availability may change repeatedly

Typical uses include notifying a monitoring system, logging reader state changes
or asking a supervised controller to restart external network equipment at
sites where connectivity is unreliable.


Test procedure:
"""""""""""""""

1. Confirm that the reader is powered, configured and reachable over the
   network:

       $ ping 192.168.17.100
       $ nc -vz 192.168.17.100 3999

2. Confirm that the reader configuration file points to the installed driver
   library:

       $ find "$(pkg-config libpcsclite --variable=usbdropdir)" \
           -path "*/ifd-springcard-ccid-tcp.bundle/Contents/*/libspringcard_ccid_tcp.*" \
           -print

3. Restart pcscd. The exact command depends on the system:

       $ sudo systemctl restart pcscd

   or run pcscd in foreground debug mode during integration:

       $ sudo pcscd --foreground --debug

4. Use pcsc_scan or your PC/SC application to verify that the configured reader
   appears and that card insertion/removal is reported.


Troubleshooting:
""""""""""""""""

- If the reader does not appear, check reader configuration syntax, LIBPATH,
  the reader IP address, TCP port and local firewall rules.
- If pcscd never reads the configuration directory, verify that your pcsc-lite
  build/package has serial reader configuration support enabled.
- If pcscd cannot load the driver, verify that the installed shared library path
  matches LIBPATH and that file permissions allow pcscd to read and execute it.
- If the hook is not called, verify that it exists and is executable.
- If pcscd logs show hook timeouts, move the slow work out of the hook and into
  a background service or monitoring system.
